{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W9ugPXzPHzhHcrc6QZ9ajF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"THE VERDICT RECORD: which artifact carries a lane's governing review verdict,\n2\tand what binds that verdict to the tree it judged.\n3\t\n4\tWHY THIS EXISTS (OI-76, ruled 2026-08-31 — \"B with C and the generated rollup;\n5\tgoverning = TREE IDENTITY\"). The merge gate used to read a hand-authored\n6\t`docs/reviews/WI-<n>-REVIEW-A.md` rollup that **nothing in the kit wrote**, and\n7\tit judged that rollup's freshness by comparing commit TIMES. Three defects rode\n8\ttogether: nobody wrote it, so every mechanized lane stopped at a human; it\n9\tparaphrased evidence that already existed; and the trust was inverted — the\n10\tround files carry the anti-forgery story and the gate ignored them. The ruling\n11\tre-points the gate at the evidence and replaces the time comparison with an\n12\tidentity: **a verdict counts only if it names the tree it judged.**\n13\t\n14\tONE DEFINITION, TWO READERS, and that is the whole reason this is a module\n15\trather than two private helpers. `integrate._verdict_gate` (may this branch\n16\tmerge?) and `agent_loop.review_owed_by_evidence` (does this lane still owe a\n17\tround?) are the same question asked from two sides, and they used to answer it\n18\twith two different rules over two different exclusion sets — which is how one\n19\tlane drew two identical `APPROVE findings=0` rounds because the loop's own\n20\ttelemetry commits had moved HEAD past a verdict that was never invalidated.\n21\t\n22\tTHE IDENTITY, and why it is not a git tree sha. `tree_identity` folds\n23\t`git ls-tree -r <rev>` with the RECORD paths removed — `docs/reviews/`,\n24\t`docs/log.d/`, `docs/iteration/`. Those three are the process writing about\n25\titself: a round file, a log fragment and a session log are records OF the work\n26\tunder review, never the work, so a commit that only touches them cannot\n27\tinvalidate a verdict. The digest is a truncation-free SHA-256 over the filtered\n28\tlisting, 64 hex — deliberately NOT 40, because a 40-hex `tree=` beside the\n29\t`Bar-Green: tree=<40 hex>` trailer (which IS a git tree object id) would read as\n30\tthe same kind of value and it is not one.\n31\t\n32\tWHAT CANNOT INVALIDATE A VERDICT HAS TWO HALVES, AND ONE OWNER. The record\n33\tPATHS above are the first; the station's REFRESH COMMIT is the second, and it is\n34\there for the same reason rather than in the merge slot. A refresh merges the\n35\ttrunk in and re-runs the compile/regen: it moves the tree without the lane\n36\thaving changed its work, so a verdict is governed by the tree at the peeled work\n37\ttip (`governing_rev`), not at the branch tip. WI-560 Done-when 1 asks for ONE\n38\tdefinition of \"the last commit that could invalidate a verdict\" — that sentence\n39\tcovers which commit as much as which paths, so `governing_identity` is the\n40\tsingle answer BOTH readers are handed. When the peel lived in `integrate` and\n41\tthe loop measured at `HEAD` instead, the two readers disagreed across exactly\n42\tone refresh commit and a resumed lane drew a round the gate would not read\n43\t(REVIEW-A round 007, finding 2).\n44\t\n45\tTHE TWO HALVES INTERACT, which is the part that had to be driven to be believed.\n46\tThe peel is written twice on purpose: `work_tip` peels only a refresh sitting\n47\tliterally on the tip, because it feeds a `reset --hard` where peeling one commit\n48\ttoo far destroys committed work, and `governing_rev` walks THROUGH record-only\n49\tcommits to reach a refresh they would otherwise hide. While the identity used\n50\tthe tip-only peel, a single telemetry commit — a record path, which the fold is\n51\tbuilt to ignore — moved the identity anyway, by burying the refresh under it. A\n52\tread-only question can afford an answer a destructive one cannot.\n53\t\n54\tTHE TRAILER is the machine half, the `Bar-Green:` pattern applied to a verdict:\n55\t\n56\t    Review-Verdict: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED rounds=<N> tree=<64 hex>\n57\t\n58\tIt rides the commit that RECORDS the round, is written by the coordinator (never\n59\tby a session), and is additive — an adopter whose loop does not write one yet\n60\tpays nothing, because the round file's own filename carries the reviewed sha and\n61\tresolves to the same identity.\n62\t\n63\tTHE LOGGED-SESSION RESTRICTION closes the plan's finding K: a BUILD session once\n64\twrote `010-REVIEW-A-e26ab03.md` into the review path — an implementer authoring\n65\tits own approval. A file in `docs/reviews/` is a ROUND only when the coordinator's\n66\town committed session log for that (train, ordinal) declares a REVIEW phase.\n67\tThe session log is written and committed by the loop, so the join is evidence a\n68\tsession cannot forge for itself without also forging the coordinator's telemetry.\n69\t\n70\tWHAT THIS MODULE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO: it never decides what an absent answer\n71\tMEANS (`kitlib.git`'s rule, inherited). `None` is \"git had nothing to say\"; the\n72\tmerge gate reads that as a refusal and the loop's derivation reads it as \"assume\n73\ta round is owed\" — both fail toward more review, and each says so at its own\n74\tcall site.\n75\t\n76\tContracts: IF-175 — the seam this module declares (process.md §8; row of record\n77\tin docs/requirements/interfaces.toml).\n78\t\n79\tContract IF-175: the verdict record, as functions two independent readers call\n80\t    to reach ONE answer. `tree_identity` (and its pure half `fold_listing`)\n81\t    gives the non-record tree identity a verdict names, and\n82\t    `governing_identity` — over `governing_rev` / `refresh_attestation` — gives\n83\t    the rev that identity is measured at, so neither reader chooses its own\n84\t    (`work_tip` is the tip-only peel the `reset --hard` shares, deliberately\n85\t    NOT the same walk);\n86\t    `format_trailer` / `parse_trailer` the `Review-Verdict:` machine half;\n87\t    `round_file` / `session_log` the two name grammars `docs/reviews/` and\n88\t    `docs/iteration/`\n89\t    carry; `branch_paths` / `logged_rounds` / `round_entries` the round evidence\n90\t    a branch holds, restricted to rounds a logged reviewer session produced and\n91\t    to the tree under judgement; `branch_trailers` the verified attestations on\n92\t    the branch's own commits, in commit order. Pure functions plus thin reads through\n93\t    `kitlib.git`, so nothing here writes and nothing imports a sibling service —\n94\t    the verdict parser arrives as an ARGUMENT rather than an import, which is\n95\t    what keeps this leaf free of an edge back into the scoring layer. Every\n96\t    function answers `None` where git had nothing to say and NEVER decides what\n97\t    that means: the merge slot reads it as a refusal, the loop's review-owed\n98\t    derivation as \"assume a round is owed\", and both directions are toward more\n99\t    review. The contract this row exists to hold is the SINGULARITY — a second\n100\t    reader of round evidence anywhere else is a finding against this row.\n101\t\"\"\"\n102\t\n103\timport hashlib\n104\timport re\n105\t\n106\tfrom .git import git_out\n107\tfrom .station import BAR_GREE
[... byte cap hit — full stream in out/run-logs/ ...]
