{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0119UWWWqxjAcZn5oXq1GBL3","type":"tool_result","content":"375\t# How far `governing_rev` will walk. Deeper than `_MAX_REFRESH_PEEL` because\n376\t# what it walks THROUGH is identity-preserving commits, and a lane accumulates\n377\t# one telemetry commit per session. Hitting the bound stops the walk, which\n378\t# measures at a later rev and so can only ask for MORE review, never less.\n379\t_MAX_GOVERNING_WALK = 64\n380\t\n381\t\n382\tdef governing_rev(root, branch):\n383\t    \"\"\"The commit whose tree governs a verdict on `branch`: the tip, with\n384\t    refresh commits peeled AND identity-preserving commits walked through.\n385\t\n386\t    WHY THIS IS NOT `work_tip`. Both peel a refresh, and they must not share an\n387\t    implementation, because `work_tip` feeds a `reset --hard` and may only ever\n388\t    peel a refresh sitting literally on the tip — peeling one commit further\n389\t    there DESTROYS committed work. This function only ever reads, so it can\n390\t    afford the honest question: is there a refresh commit under here that\n391\t    nothing invalidating has been stacked on?\n392\t\n393\t    That gap was a live defect, driven. The fold already ignores\n394\t    `RECORD_PREFIXES`, so a telemetry commit cannot move the identity — but a\n395\t    telemetry commit landing ON TOP of a refresh made the tip stop being a\n396\t    refresh commit, the tip-only peel stopped applying, and the identity flipped\n397\t    from the pre-refresh work tree to the post-refresh one. An APPROVE served\n398\t    before the refresh then named a tree nothing governed: the loop answered\n399\t    `owed=True` and the merge slot refused \"no logged review round names its\n400\t    current tree\" — both readers agreeing on the WRONG answer, and an honest\n401\t    approval parked at a supervisor stop by the coordinator's own telemetry.\n402\t    That is the OI-76 failure mode this row exists to eliminate, surviving one\n403\t    commit further down than the round-007 fix reached.\n404\t\n405\t    THE STEP CONDITION IS THE DEFINITION ITSELF, not a proxy for it. A commit\n406\t    may be walked through exactly when its non-record identity EQUALS its first\n407\t    parent's — which is, in as many words, \"a commit that cannot invalidate a\n408\t    verdict\", the sentence this whole module is built around. Measuring it\n409\t    directly is what makes the walk provably identity-neutral: `governing_rev`\n410\t    can only ever return a rev with the identity the tip already had, so the\n411\t    walk cannot invent an answer, only see PAST commits to a refresh they would\n412\t    otherwise hide.\n413\t\n414\t    It replaces a predicate that CLASSIFIED THE PATHS a commit touched, and the\n415\t    replacement is the fix for round 012's finding 1 rather than a tidy-up. That\n416\t    predicate had to answer for every commit shape whose paths it could not\n417\t    read — a merge, an empty commit — and it answered \"stop\", which reads as\n418\t    \"this might have invalidated the verdict\" for a commit that provably did\n419\t    not. `agent_common.commit_telemetry` then began writing exactly that shape:\n420\t    a `Review-Verdict:` attestation must land even when the bookkeeping it rides\n421\t    is unchanged, so it commits EMPTY, and the very commit that RECORDS an\n422\t    approval buried the refresh underneath it. Asking the identity question\n423\t    directly makes the empty carrier, the merge commit and the quoted-path trap\n424\t    (`is_record_path` against git's display encoding, round 007's finding 3\n425\t    pointed the more dangerous way round) all unrepresentable at once, because\n426\t    no path is classified here at all.\"\"\"\n427\t    rev = branch\n428\t    identity = tree_identity(root, rev)\n429\t    for _ in range(_MAX_GOVERNING_WALK):"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8d6eacf-e9a3-4de4-bdd7-43742f777ec0","uuid":"43629022-c24f-4443-a460-5b671dfe7157","timestamp":"2026-09-03T04:45:01.384Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/diytechy/Documents/ai-template-drive/wi-579-the-verdict-carrier-and-the-ad/project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py","content":"# How far `governing_rev` will walk. Deeper than `_MAX_REFRESH_PEEL` because\n# what it walks THROUGH is identity-preserving commits, and a lane accumulates\n# one telemetry commit per session. Hitting the bound stops the walk, which\n# measures at a later rev and so can only ask for MORE review, never less.\n_MAX_GOVERNING_WALK = 64\n\n\ndef governing_rev(root, branch):\n    \"\"\"The commit whose tree governs a verdict on `branch`: the tip, with\n    refresh commits peeled AND identity-preserving commits walked through.\n\n    WHY THIS IS NOT `work_tip`. Both peel a refresh, and they must not share an\n    implementation, because `work_tip` feeds a `reset --hard` and may only ever\n    peel a refresh sitting literally on the tip — peeling one commit further\n    there DESTROYS committed work. This function only ever reads, so it can\n    afford the honest question: is there a refresh commit under here that\n    nothing invalidating has been stacked on?\n\n    That gap was a live defect, driven. The fold already ignores\n    `RECORD_PREFIXES`, so a telemetry commit cannot move the identity — but a\n    telemetry commit landing ON TOP of a refresh made the tip stop being a\n    refresh commit, the tip-only peel stopped applying, and the identity flipped\n    from the pre-refresh work tree to the post-refresh one. An APPROVE served\n    before the refresh then named a tree nothing governed: the loop answered\n    `owed=True` and the merge slot refused \"no logged review round names its\n    current tree\" — both readers agreeing on the WRONG answer, and an honest\n    approval parked at a supervisor stop by the coordinator's own telemetry.\n    That is the OI-76 failure mode this row exists to eliminate, surviving one\n    commit further down than the round-007 fix reached.\n\n    THE STEP CONDITION IS THE DEFINITION ITSELF, not a proxy for it. A commit\n    may be walked through exactly when its non-record identity EQUALS its first\n    parent's — which is, in as many words, \"a commit that cannot invalidate a\n    verdict\", the sentence this whole module is built around. Measuring it\n    directly is what makes the walk provably identity-neutral: `governing_rev`\n    can only ever return a rev with the identity the tip already had, so the\n    walk cannot invent an answer, only see PAST commits to a refresh they would\n    otherwise hide.\n\n    It replaces a predicate that CLASSIFIED THE PATHS a commit touched, and the\n    replacement is the fix for round 012's finding 1 rather than a tidy-up. That\n    predicate had to answer for every commit shape whose paths it could not\n    read — a merge, an empty commit — and it answered \"stop\", which reads as\n    \"this might have invalidated the verdict\" for a commit that provably did\n    not. `agent_common.commit_telemetry` then began writing exactly that shape:\n    a `Review-Verdict:` attestation must land even when the bookkeeping it rides\n    is unchanged, so it commits EMPTY, and the very commit that RECORDS an\n    approval buried the refresh underneath it. Asking the identity question\n    directly makes the empty carrier, the merge commit and the quoted-path trap\n    (`is_record_path` against git's display encoding, round 007's finding 3\n    pointed the more dangerous way round) all unrepresentable at once, because\n    no path is classified here at all.\"\"\"\n    rev = branch\n    identity = tree_identity(root, rev)\n    for _ in range(_MAX_GOVERNING_WALK):","numLines":55,"startLine":375,"totalLines":671}}}
